    <h1>Fan-tailed Raven Nesting: Breeding and Nest Sites</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Fan-tailed Raven (<em>Corvus rhipidurus</em>) nests on cliff faces and in rocky gorges across the Horn of Africa, Red Sea coast, and Arabian Peninsula. The nest is a stick platform on a ledge or in a crevice. Breeding season varies by region: approximately November–April in the Horn of Africa; January–April in Arabia.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nest Sites</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Fan-tailed Raven is strongly cliff-associated throughout its range:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Primary sites:</strong> ledges, crevices, and cave entrances on steep cliff faces in rocky gorges, escarpments, and rocky hillsides in arid and semi-arid zones.</li>
      <li><strong>Range:</strong> Somalia, Ethiopia, Eritrea, Djibouti, Yemen, Oman, Saudi Arabia, Jordan, and Israel — essentially the arid zone from the Horn of Africa to the southern Levant.</li>
      <li><strong>Communal roosting:</strong> outside the breeding season, Fan-tailed Ravens often roost communally on cliff faces, sometimes in large groups — behaviour linked to their strong cliff fidelity.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Pairs maintain long-term bonds and return to the same cliff and often the same ledge in successive breeding seasons, adding to and enlarging the nest structure each year.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nest Structure and Eggs</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The nest is a platform of sticks and branches on a cliff ledge or in a crevice, lined with softer materials including wool, hair, and plant fibres. Clutch size: typically 3–5 eggs, pale blue-green to greenish-white with brown and grey blotching — typical of large corvids. Incubation: approximately 20–21 days. Post-fledging dependence lasts several weeks; young birds learn cliff foraging behaviour from parents.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Breeding Season</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Breeding timing varies across the range:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Horn of Africa (Ethiopia, Somalia, Eritrea):</strong> breeding documented November–April — the drier season in the region.</li>
      <li><strong>Arabian Peninsula (Yemen, Oman, Saudi Arabia):</strong> January–April, coinciding with the cooler, drier months.</li>
      <li><strong>Levant (Jordan, Israel):</strong> February–May.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>As with other corvids in arid zones, breeding tends to be timed to avoid the hottest and least productive periods of the year.</p>
  </section>

